  "prompt_text": "The figure is a map of the British Isles and nearby northwestern Europe showing major high-voltage electricity interconnectors linking Great Britain and Ireland to neighboring countries. Red lines trace cable routes across the sea, with solid lines indicating operational links and dashed lines indicating projects in development. Line thickness represents transmission capacity, ranging from about 500 MW to 2000 MW.\n\nOverall, the map emphasizes the growing cross-border power network centered on Britain. Existing connections cluster most strongly in the English Channel and southern North Sea, where Britain is already linked to France, Belgium, and the Netherlands through lines such as IFA, ElecLink, NEMO, and BritNed. There are also westward connections between Great Britain and Ireland, including EWIC and Greenlink, and a shorter link between Scotland and Northern Ireland labeled Moyle.\n\nThe proposed projects extend this network much farther. Dashed routes show planned large-capacity links from eastern England to Norway (NSL) and to Denmark (Viking), as well as additional France connections labeled IFA2 and FAB Link. Visually, the figure conveys both the current concentration of interconnection in southern Britain and the planned expansion toward Scandinavia and continental Europe.",
